        "math_academy_id": "q-99517",
        "problem": "If ${F}^{\u2032}(t)=\u27e83(t-1)^{2},4\\,{t}^{3}-1\u27e9$ and $F(1)=\u27e81,\\,1\u27e9,$ then $F(t)=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \u27e83(t-1)^{2},\\,4{t}^{3}-1\u27e9\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \u27e83(t-1)^{2},\\,4{t}^{3}-1\u27e9\\text{d}t & =\u27e8\\int 3(t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (4{t}^{3}-1)\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{(t-1)}^{3}+{c}_{1},\\,{t}^{4}-t+{c}_{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe initial condition $F(1)=\u27e81,\\,1\u27e9$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1)=\u27e8{((1)-1)}^{3}+{c}_{1},\\,(1)^{4}-(1)+{c}_{2}\u27e9 & =\u27e81,\\,1\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e8{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}\u27e9 & =\u27e81,\\,1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=\u27e8{(t-1)}^{3}+1,\\,{t}^{4}-t+1\u27e9.\n$$",
